<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Continental Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Continental Congress was the assembly of delegates from the Thirteen Colonies that directed American resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and then governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1774; the Second, from 1775, created the Continental Army, adopted the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776 and drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>. It continued as the Congress of the Confederation until 1789.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/continental-congress.jpg" alt="Continental Congress" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Édouard Armand-Dumaresq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signing_of_Declaration_of_Independence_by_Armand-Dumaresq,_c1873_-_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
